# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in your key.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and never committed. The npm scripts that can spend money
# load it via `node --env-file-if-exists=.env`, so no shell restart is needed --
# just create the file and run `npm run smoke`.
#
# Nothing in `npm test` reads this file. The test script is a bare `node --test`,
# so the key is never in the process during a test run. That is deliberate: it is
# one of four independent guards against a test suite spending money.
#
# Get a key at https://fal.ai/dashboard/keys
FAL_KEY=your-fal-key-here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase. Only `npm run web` reads these -- Google sign-in, the six-digit
# email confirmation code, and password reset all go through them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All three or none. `npm run web` boots either way -- with none of the three
# set, /login, /signup and /auth/google answer 503 and the rest of the app
# still serves; with only some set, the startup banner prints a loud warning
# naming which are missing rather than refusing to start (see
# scripts/web/server-cli.mjs's `describeSupabaseConfig`).
#
# SUPABASE_URL is this project's own API base, e.g.
# https://<project-ref>.supabase.co -- find it on the project's API settings
# page.
SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project-ref.supabase.co
#
# SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY is safe to expose to a browser (this app never
# does; it has no client JavaScript by rule) -- it is used for the calls that
# do not need elevation.
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_your-key-here
#
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY is REQUIRED, not optional: Supabase Auth rate-limits
# per IP address, and a server-side app calling the auth endpoints on
# everybody's behalf presents ONE IP. Without this key, every user of this
# deployment shares a single rate-limit bucket keyed on the server's own
# address, and one person retrying a login enough times locks out everybody
# else. The remedy is the
# `Sb-Forwarded-For` header carrying the END USER's real address -- and
# Supabase only honours that header on a request made with the secret key.
# It confers admin over the auth service (it can create and delete users), so
# treat it exactly like FAL_KEY and STRIPE_SECRET_KEY below: it must never be
# logged, never rendered, and never leave this file for a browser.
#
# NEVER PUT A REAL VALUE IN THIS FILE. It is `.env.example`, not `.env` --
# committed to a public repository -- and every value above is a placeholder
# to overwrite, not a credential to paste.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_your-key-here

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe. Only `npm run web` reads these, and only for buying credit packs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# TEST-MODE KEYS FIRST. A live secret key in this file is a live secret key on
# a laptop; `sk_test_` and a test-mode webhook secret prove the whole path
# without a real card ever existing.
#
# The card is entered on Stripe's hosted page, on Stripe's domain. This
# repository never sees a card number and has no field to type one into.
#
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is sent to api.stripe.com and to nowhere else -- the host is
# a constant in scripts/billing/stripe.mjs with no parameter that can move it,
# which is the fix for finding F3 of the security review applied in advance.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_your-stripe-test-key-here
#
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET is what proves a webhook delivery really came from
# Stripe. Without it every delivery is answered 503 and NOTHING is granted --
# deliberately, because a 200 would tell Stripe to stop retrying a payment this
# app failed to credit. `stripe listen` prints one:
#
#   stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
#
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_your-webhook-signing-secret-here
#
# Where this app is reachable from, for the two urls Stripe redirects a buyer
# back to after checkout. NEVER derived from the request's Host header, because
# that is a value the caller chooses. Unset is fine locally: it falls back to
# the address the server bound to.
# TIMESTAMP_PUBLIC_URL=https://timestamp.example
#
# Whether `x-forwarded-proto` decides the Secure attribute on cookies -- and,
# since 2026-08-29, which address the auth rate limiters key on. Leave it
# unset until this app runs behind a TLS-terminating proxy the deployment
# actually has -- the header is whatever the client typed unless such a proxy
# rewrote it. Setting it to 1 is the operator vouching for that proxy, which
# must overwrite inbound `x-forwarded-for` (Caddy's default for untrusted
# peers) or the leftmost entry is client-typed on every route that reads it.
# TIMESTAMP_TRUST_PROXY=1
#
# Which provider the WEB process labels new jobs with, and -- since 2026-08-29
# -- what decides whether a web job renders DIRECT (photograph straight to
# tape, no still stage). A paid provider here means direct; the fixture keeps
# the still path the dev loop renders. The worker's own --provider flag is
# what actually spends; these two must agree, and in production BOTH are fal.
# Unset falls back to `fixture`, which in production would send every order
# down the still path into the deliberately-unverified model's refusal at
# compose -- loud, refunded, and wrong. Set it on the day the worker runs fal.
# TIMESTAMP_PROVIDER=fal
#
# Who is selling, for /privacy, /terms and /impressum -- a JSON object on ONE
# line. It belongs here rather than in config/legal.json because a sole
# trader's disclosure address is their HOME address, and this file is
# gitignored, kept out of the image by .dockerignore, and already passed into
# both containers by compose's `env_file`. The pages publish the address while
# the site is live either way; what this avoids is a public repository's
# permanent history, which outlives any page you can take down.
#
# All four fields are required except vatId, which may be null. A malformed or
# half-filled value is REFUSED and the pages fall back to the operator
# placeholder with the reason in the log -- so check /impressum after deploy
# (the runbook's smoke list step 7), because a placeholder is what a silently
# rejected value looks like.
#
# Leaving it unset falls back to config/legal.json, whose `entity: null` is the
# designed pre-launch state.
# TIMESTAMP_LEGAL_ENTITY={"name":"Your Name","addressLines":["Street","City PIN","Country"],"email":"support@timestamptapes.com","vatId":null}
#
# Whether search engines may index this site. UNSET MEANS NO, and that default
# is deliberate: the Impressum carries an address at which documents can be
# served, which for a sole trader is a home address, and an indexed and
# archived home address cannot be withdrawn. Forgetting to switch this ON costs
# search traffic, which is visible and fixable any day; forgetting to switch it
# OFF is permanent. The recoverable mistake gets to be the default.
#
# Note that not linking the site is NOT a substitute. Caddy issues a
# certificate on first boot and that puts the hostname into public Certificate
# Transparency logs, which crawlers watch -- the site is findable from the
# first TLS handshake.
#
# Set it to 1 on the day you want to be found. Job pages and their media stay
# disallowed in robots.txt either way: a tape is somebody's face.
# TIMESTAMP_INDEXABLE=1

# --- image moderation (optional, OFF unless all three are set) ---------------
#
# Amazon Rekognition DetectModerationLabels, called by the WORKER before any
# paid render, to check the photograph a customer uploaded. Unset, the seam
# stays null and `moderate.mjs` records an `image-unclassified` warning in the
# manifest -- "nobody looked at these pixels" -- which is how this product has
# shipped all along and is an honest state rather than a broken one.
#
# SET ALL THREE OR NONE. Two of three is refused at worker startup and the
# process exits, on purpose: a worker that starts having quietly decided not to
# check photographs is worse than one that refuses to start.
#
# Cost is about $0.001 an image, and the check runs at step 2 of 11 -- before
# the money -- so a refusal costs nothing.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, because the name invites the assumption. It does not
# detect CSAM; AWS says so in its own documentation, and known-CSAM detection
# is hash matching against a vetted database (PhotoDNA, Project Arachnid's
# Shield, Google CSAI Match) which is a programme you apply to. It also cannot
# tell whether the person in the photograph agreed to be in it -- the consent
# checkbox remains the only control on that.
#
# BEFORE TURNING THIS ON, THE PRIVACY PAGE HAS TO CHANGE. It currently says the
# photograph goes to fal.ai "and to nobody else", which stops being true the
# moment this is configured. AWS also becomes a processor: it needs a DPA and a
# line in the processor list. eu-central-1 keeps the photograph in Frankfurt,
# which is the same jurisdiction as the Hetzner box already named on that page.
# AWS_REGION=eu-central-1
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN=
